MID_NR23_00_m01: <SH>
MID_NR23_00_m01_001: Dagdan Imperial Calendar, year 1450.
MID_NR23_00_m01_002: After the Heroic Games, the Divine Emperor Dagda\nawoke, retook his throne, and began a new year with\nbold proclamations. He named Lord Solel a traitor,\nhis reign invalid, and declared war on Saramis.
MID_NR23_00_m01_003: The emperor's statements shook the entire continent.\nPeople everywhere feared what would come next.\nQueen Theodora rose up in defense of the Saramis\nnation, stoking open rebellion against the empire.
MID_NR23_00_m01_004: Theodora sought allies for her sudden war. She sent\nemissaries far and wide, hoping that others would\njoin her cause and bolster her forces.
MID_NR23_00_m01_005: Many of them joined her rebellion. One powerful\nwarrior after another took their place at Theodora's\nside, ready to defend her people.
MID_NR23_00_m01_006: Theodora led the march against the empire, standing\nat the fore in battle after battle. No matter the risk,\nno matter how fierce the fight, she cleaved a path\nto victory. The queen was a true inspiration.
MID_NR23_00_m01_007: Then in Efraul of that year, the Alliance was finally\nable to march on Dagsion itself. After winning the\nBattle of Alcineus Span, they pushed onward, to\nchallenge the Divine Emperor inside his city.
MID_NR23_00_m01_008: But as they entered Dagsion, not one of them knew\nwhat had lurked behind the throne all along—or of\nthe nightmare about to dawn over the land of Dagda.
MID_NR23_00_m01_009: —Saint Cichol, "History of the Dagdan War,"\nForeword
MID_NR21_00_m01: <SH>
MID_NR21_00_m01_001: Dagdan Imperial Calendar, year 1450.
MID_NR21_00_m01_002: After the Heroic Games, the Divine Emperor Dagda\nawoke, retook his throne, and began a new year with\nbold proclamations. He named Lord Solel a traitor,\nhis reign invalid, and declared war on Saramis.
MID_NR21_00_m01_003: The emperor's statements shook the entire continent.\nPeople everywhere feared what would come next.\nQueen Theodora rose up in defense of the Saramis\nnation, stoking open rebellion against the empire.
MID_NR21_00_m01_004: She sent an emissary to ask Cai to join her cause.\nHe and his family had recently settled into their\nnew life in a house on the edge of Dagsion.
MID_NR21_00_m01_005: He was resolved to help Theodora, feeling a strong\nand lasting bond with the queen. So, with his\nfamily's blessing, Cai went off to fight in the war.
MID_NR21_00_m01_006: Cai quickly proved himself to be an invaluable\nmember of the Alliance. Even the elite Saramis\nwarriors remarked on his superior combat skills.\nAnd his battle successes were spectacular.
MID_NR21_00_m01_007: Then in Efraul of that year, the Alliance was finally\nable to march on Dagsion itself. After winning the\nBattle of Alcineus Span, they pushed onward, to\nchallenge the Divine Emperor inside his city.
MID_NR21_00_m01_008: But as they entered Dagsion, not one of them knew\nwhat had lurked behind the throne all along—or of\nthe nightmare about to dawn over the land of Dagda.
MID_NR21_00_m01_009: —Saint Cichol, "History of the Dagdan War,"\nForeword
MID_NR22_00_m01: <SH>
MID_NR22_00_m01_001: Dagdan Imperial Calendar, year 1450.
MID_NR22_00_m01_002: After the Heroic Games, the Divine Emperor Dagda\nawoke, retook his throne, and began a new year with\nbold proclamations. He named Lord Solel a traitor,\nhis reign invalid, and declared war on Saramis.
MID_NR22_00_m01_003: The emperor's statements shook the entire continent.\nPeople everywhere feared what would come next.\nQueen Theodora rose up in defense of the Saramis\nnation, stoking open rebellion against the empire.
MID_NR22_00_m01_004: Theodora sent an emissary to ask Dietrich to join her\ncause. After his fierce battles in the Heroic Games,\nhe'd stayed awhile longer in the city of Dagsion.
MID_NR22_00_m01_005: Dietrich received this call to action in his unique\nway, overcome with the urge to cut down Dagda the\nGreat. He joined the Saramis war immediately.
MID_NR22_00_m01_006: He relished each and every opportunity during the\ncampaign to slaughter the enemy. The Saramis \nsoldiers were relieved—deeply—that someone with\nsuch a demonic disposition was their ally in battle.
MID_NR22_00_m01_007: Then in Efraul of that year, the Alliance was finally\nable to march on Dagsion itself. After winning the\nBattle of Alcineus Span, they pushed onward, to\nchallenge the Divine Emperor inside his city.
MID_NR22_00_m01_008: But as they entered Dagsion, not one of them knew\nwhat had lurked behind the throne all along—or of\nthe nightmare about to dawn over the land of Dagda.
MID_NR22_00_m01_009: —Saint Cichol, "History of the Dagdan War,"\nForeword
MID_NR24_00_m01: <SH>
MID_NR24_00_m01_001: Dagdan Imperial Calendar, year 1450.
MID_NR24_00_m01_002: After the Heroic Games, the Divine Emperor Dagda\nawoke, retook his throne, and began a new year with\nbold proclamations. He named Lord Solel a traitor,\nhis reign invalid, and declared war on Saramis.
MID_NR24_00_m01_003: The emperor's statements shook the entire continent.\nPeople everywhere feared what would come next.\nQueen Theodora rose up in defense of the Saramis\nnation, stoking open rebellion against the empire.
MID_NR24_00_m01_004: She sent an emissary to ask Leda to join her cause.\nLeda was preparing for a journey south—to find a\nway to save her father's soul.
MID_NR24_00_m01_005: The Divine Emperor's edicts were already striking\nher as tyranny in motion. So, strongly opposed,\nLeda went off to fight in the Saramis rebellion.
MID_NR24_00_m01_006: Her performances were the secret to the army's\nstrong morale during the war campaign. Obsidian\nseemed to eat at her very soul. But Leda played on,\nbattle after battle, inspiring the troops to keep going.
MID_NR24_00_m01_007: Then in Efraul of that year, the Alliance was finally\nable to march on Dagsion itself. After winning the\nBattle of Alcineus Span, they pushed onward, to\nchallenge the Divine Emperor inside his city.
MID_NR24_00_m01_008: But as they entered Dagsion, not one of them knew\nwhat had lurked behind the throne all along—or of\nthe nightmare about to dawn over the land of Dagda.
MID_NR24_00_m01_009: —Saint Cichol, "History of the Dagdan War,"\nForeword
